# Approach to Renal Masses - Literature Search on UpToDate <span class="tag" tag-name="Reference"><span class="smallcaps">Reference</span></span>

- **Background and rationale** - RCC is considered the 'radiologist's tumour' because it is usually diagnosed through incidental finding on abdominal imaging
- **Terminology:**
  - <u>Renal cysts</u> - cystic lesions of the kidneys which may be simple or complex based on Bosniak classification
  - <u>Renal mass</u> - solid or cystic lesions identified on multiphase, cross-sectional abdominal imaging w/ or w/out contrast enhancement
- **Classification of solid renal mass** - increasing tumour size is best predictor of malignancy, although not definite:
  - <u>Large renal mass</u> (\> 4cm) - generally \> 4cm solid mass lesion has high PPV for RCC (at least T1b) esp. if compatible radiological features and hence **must be managed as RCC**
  - <u>Small renal mass</u> (\< 4cm) - defined as a kidney lesion \< 4cm in largest diameter on high-quality, multiphase, cross-sectional abdominal imaging w/ or w/o contrast enhancement, and **presents with clinical uncertainty influencing Mx**
- **DDx of small renal masses {SRM}:**
  - **Renal cell carcinoma** - most contrast-enhancing turns out to are renal cell carcinoma
  - **Benign kidney tumour** - smaller tumours raises a possibility of a potentially benign kidney tumour:
    - <u>Oncocytoma</u> - homogenous well-circumscribed solid mass w/ central scar
    - <u>Angiomyolipoma</u> - enhancing mass that contains macroscopic fat and no calcifications (bilateral angiomyolipoma points towards tuberous sclerosis)
    - <u>Metanephric adenoma</u> - rare benign lesion of the kidneys
  - **Metastatic disease of the kidney** - typically in a patient undergoing surveillance after definitive Tx of another malignancy, presenting with multiple kidney lesions rather than solitary lesion (Hx of prior malignancy does not exclude RCC)
  - **Lymphoma** - haematological malignancy within the kidneys is possible, although other clinical features (e.g. generalised lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly) should be present
  - **Renal abscess** - typically dominated by other clinical features such as fever, chills, flank pain and pyuria
- **Mx of SRM:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - **Considerations for Mx of SRM** - RCC is slow growing and risk of metastasis is slow in early phases of the natural Hx:
      - <u>Tumour factors</u> - tumour size (\< 3cm vs 3-4cm), rapid growth rate (\> 5mm/y), imaging characteristics, and tumour location
      - <u>Patient factors</u> - patient age, life-expectancy, comorbidities (incl. baseline renal functions)
    - **Mx options:**
      - <u>Partial nephrectomy</u> - nephron-preservation with surgical removal of tumour is preferred for surgical candidates due to favourable oncological outcome while minimises risk of progressive CKD
      - <u>Ablative techniques</u> (Cryotherapy or RFA) - reserved for smaller SRMs (\<3cm) for patients who do not contemplate or cannot tolerate definitive surgery
      - <u>Active surveillance</u> - for all SRM \< 1cm, and as alternative approach to non-surgical candidates w/ SRM \> 1cm (1-4cm)
